The '''Foneria General de Balboa''' (FGB; {{lang|ca|Foneria General de Balboa}}; "General Foundry of Balboa") was a Balboan {{w|ironworks}} and {{w|foundry}} established in 1903 at [[Les Drassanes]], near [[Portnou]], during the [[Zong Balboa]] colonial period. Founded by Volisanian metallurgist [[Esteve Camps i Ribera]], the FGB was the first steel-producing facility in the Balboan isthmus and the predecessor of the modern [[Acereries de l'Istme]] (ACI), the largest steel company in Balboa.

The FGB operated for over four decades, supplying {{w|pig iron}}, {{w|cast iron}}, and {{w|steel}} products to the [[Kingdom of Zong|Zong]] navy and the Zong Balboa colonial administration. The company was destroyed during the [[Great Adonian War]] (1935–1947) and formally dissolved in 1949, when its remaining assets were absorbed into the state-owned [[Acereria Nacional de Balboa]] (ANB).

== History ==

=== Founding (1903) ===

The FGB was founded in 1903 by '''Esteve Camps i Ribera''', a Volisanian metallurgist from the colonial settler community in the [[Zong Balboa]] territory. Camps had previously worked at a Volisanian ironworks in [[Ampuria]] and emigrated to the Zong Balboa territory shortly after the [[Second War of the Isthmus]] (1893–1899), which had transferred the [[Balboa Canal]] and the surrounding territory — including the western terminus at [[Portnou]] — from [[Balboa]] to the [[Kingdom of Zong]].

Camps recognised that the Zong administration's expansion of the canal zone would require a domestic source of steel for naval shipbuilding, dockyard construction, and military infrastructure. The [[Les Drassanes]] area, northwest of Portnou, was already developing as a shipbuilding and heavy industry centre, with the shipyards and dry docks that gave the municipality its name. Camps selected a site at Les Drassanes adjacent to the shipyards, with access to the bulk cargo facilities of the [[Port de Portnou]] for importing raw materials.

The FGB was established with the backing of the Zong Balboa colonial administration, which granted the company land at Les Drassanes and preferential access to Zong naval contracts. The administration's support reflected the strategic importance of a domestic steel supply for the Zong military presence in the canal zone, which included a naval base at Portnou and garrison installations along the canal corridor.

=== Early operations (1903–1914) ===

The FGB initially operated a single {{w|blast furnace}} using {{w|iron ore}} imported from [[Louyang]] — then a Zong dependency with substantial iron ore deposits — and {{w|coking coal}} imported from [[Kingdom of Zong|Zong]]. The company produced basic {{w|pig iron}}, {{w|cast iron}}, and small quantities of {{w|carbon steel|mild steel}} for the Zong navy.

In its early years, the FGB's workforce was small — approximately 200 employees in 1903 — and predominantly Volisanian, with a small number of Sinchewese technicians recruited from the Zongosphere community. The company's initial output was modest, producing primarily pig iron and cast iron for naval ordnance, dockyard fittings, and canal infrastructure.

The FGB's relationship with the Zong navy was central to its early business. The Zong administration's investment in the canal zone — including the expansion of the Portnou naval base, the construction of fortifications, and the upgrading of dockyard facilities — created a steady demand for iron and steel products that the FGB was uniquely positioned to supply as the only domestic producer.

=== Expansion and the Shining Star era (1914–1935) ===

During the 1910s and 1920s, the FGB expanded significantly, paralleling the broader prosperity of the Zong Balboa territory during the so-called "[[Zong Balboa#Reforms and the Shining Star era (1910–1935)|Shining Star era]]". The company added a second {{w|blast furnace}} and an {{w|open hearth furnace}} shop, increasing its capacity to produce higher-grade steel products.

The FGB's workforce grew from approximately 200 in 1903 to over 1,500 by 1930. The workforce composition shifted significantly during this period: while the technical and management positions remained predominantly Volisanian, the majority of the production workforce was drawn from the Black Balboan population that had migrated to the Zong Balboa territory during the Shining Star era, attracted by the higher wages and legal protections offered under Governor [[Lîm Bûn-cheng]]'s egalitarian reforms. The FGB also employed a significant number of Sinchewese workers, particularly in the rolling mill and machine shop.

The FGB's production reached approximately 150,000 tonnes of {{w|crude steel}} per year by the mid-1930s — modest by international standards but significant for the isthmus region. The company's product range had expanded to include:

* '''{{w|Steel plate}}''': For naval vessel construction at the Les Drassanes shipyards and for dockyard expansions at Portnou.
* '''Structural steel''': For dockyard construction, fortification works, and canal infrastructure.
* '''Ordnance steel''': For coastal fortification guns and naval gun components.
* '''{{w|Rail track|Railway rails}} and {{w|locomotive}} components''': For the Zong-administered Isthmus Railway, which connected Portnou to Sant Cristòfor and the canal corridor.

The FGB's relationship with the Zong military made it a strategic asset during the interwar period. As the Zong military expanded its presence in the canal zone, the company's production was increasingly directed toward military applications, including naval vessel construction, fortification reinforcement, and ordnance manufacturing.

=== Great Adonian War and destruction (1935–1947) ===

The [[Great Adonian War]] (1935–1947) devastated the FGB. The [[Balboa Campaign]] of 1935, in which Volisanian and Balboan forces assaulted the Zong Balboa territory, opened with the overrunning of the canal zone — including the installations at [[Portnou]] and [[Les Drassanes]] — in the opening weeks of the campaign. The city of [[Sant Cristòfor]], protected by its fortification walls and the Zong naval presence, held out for the duration of the war, but the canal zone was lost.

The FGB's works at Les Drassanes fell under Volisanian-Balboan control during the early phase of the war. The occupying forces attempted to operate the iron works for their own military production, but the facility's dependence on imported iron ore from [[Louyang]] — which was disrupted by the naval conflict — and coking coal from [[Kingdom of Zong|Zong]] — which was severed by the loss of the canal zone — made sustained operations difficult.

In 1942, Zong and Sinchew forces launched a northern counter-offensive that temporarily recaptured portions of the canal corridor, including the Les Drassanes area. The subsequent [[Zong occupation of Balboa (1943–1947)|Zong occupation]] of the interior lasted until the end of the war, but the fighting over the canal zone produced extensive damage to the FGB's facilities.

The Les Drassanes iron works was severely damaged during the fighting. The blast furnaces were sabotaged by retreating Zong forces in 1947, and the open hearth shop was destroyed by aerial bombardment. By the end of the war, the FGB's production had fallen to zero. The company's Volisanian management had fled, and the works sat idle.

=== Dissolution (1949) ===

In 1949, the [[Adonian Community]] — which was preparing to assume administration of the former Zong Balboa territory — provided reconstruction loans to restart the Balboan steel industry. The Balboan rump state government contributed matching funds, and the two parties established the '''[[Acereria Nacional de Balboa]]''' (ANB) — the National Steel Company of Balboa — as a state-owned conglomerate to consolidate the remaining steel assets.

The ANB absorbed the ruins of the FGB, and the company was formally dissolved. The Camps family, descendants of the founder, received no compensation for the expropriated assets, as the company's close association with the Zong colonial administration made it politically untenable for the new administration to recognise private claims. However, the Camps family would later acquire a 10 per cent stake in [[Acereries de l'Istme]] (ACI) when the ANB was privatised in 1982, restoring a connection to the family's industrial heritage.

The ANB rebuilt the Les Drassanes works with modern equipment imported from Volisania and Breisland, and production resumed in 1953. The rebuilt works would eventually become the core of ACI's operations after the 1982 privatisation.

== Legacy ==

The FGB's legacy is primarily as the founding institution of the Balboan steel industry. The company's original site at [[Les Drassanes]] remains the location of the largest steel plant in Balboa, operated by [[Acereries de l'Istme]] (ACI), and the Les Drassanes municipality continues to be a centre of heavy industry and shipbuilding.

The [[Camps family]]'s connection to the steel industry was restored in 1982, when descendants of Esteve Camps i Ribera acquired a 10 per cent stake in ACI during the privatisation of the ANB. The family remains represented on ACI's board.

